# id  tag  kind  t_s  alt_km  L_si_W  flag  note
1 - first_light 0.0030 100.000 3.5018e+06 info  L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
2 A ablation_onset 0.6750 91.899 1.5188e+07 stylized  Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~92 km as low-confidence. Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin, volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
3 F flare 0.7204 91.353 2.2846e+07 stylized  Light rises x2.6 in 0.15 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: no breakup within 0.05 s (growth of existing pancakes). Dust groups supply 0% of the rise and 0% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
4 Y first_yield 3.4710 58.659 1.1499e+10 physical  Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 0.3 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5 / H_f in [R22]).
5 F flare 3.5997 57.148 3.3619e+10 stylized  Light rises x32.0 in 0.32 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 0 at 58.6 km (99% of m0); split gen 0 at 55.8 km (99% of m0); yield gen 1 at 55.4 km (38% of m0). Dust groups supply 86% of the rise and 84% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
6 B first_breakup 3.7080 55.878 7.0884e+10 stylized  Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification. Light grew x6.2 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
7 F flare 3.9877 52.639 4.7855e+11 stylized  Light rises x1.3 in 0.09 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 1 at 53.0 km (19% of m0); split gen 1 at 52.6 km (36% of m0); yield gen 2 at 52.0 km (14% of m0) +1 more. Dust groups supply 79% of the rise and 67% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
8 b bump 4.5278 46.523 1.0837e+12 stylized  Small fluctuation, 16% above the local trend; coincides with a split (gen 2) at 46.2 km. The fine structure of this phase is set by the cascade rules, so its pattern is not predictive of a real event.
9 P peak 4.5360 46.433 1.0883e+12 physical  Maximum light. It sits ~-0.3 km from the peak of energy deposition (46.8 km) because the luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 26.3 km/s).
10 D rolloff 4.8835 42.864 3.9996e+11 physical  Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 0.35 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used up (speed 78% of v0, 19% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
11 - drag_overtakes_ablation 5.4911 38.629 1.5994e+10 stylized  Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14 km/s (from the model's heating law).
12 T tail 6.1631 35.513 1.0827e+09 low-confidence  Below 1e-3 of the peak the light is controlled by groups being retired when they slow below v_dark = 3.0 km/s and by the tau taper (2-4 km/s). Do not interpret structure below this level.
